HA control plane upgrade

The flow (one node at a time, never in parallel):

):CP-1 (first)	upgrade kubeadm package → kubeadm upgrade plan → kubeadm upgrade apply v1.30.x → drain → upgrade kubelet and kubectl → restart kubelet → uncordon

):CP-2, CP-3...	upgrade kubeadm package → kubeadm upgrade node (no plan, no apply) → drain → upgrade kubelet and kubectl → restart kubelet → uncordon

):Workers	upgrade kubeadm package → kubeadm upgrade node → drain (run from a control plane) → upgrade kubelet and kubectl → restart kubelet → uncordon

):Why apply on only the first node? apply does the cluster-wide work once: it updates the cluster config stored in the kubeadm-config ConfigMap and upgrades the shared addons (CoreDNS, kube-proxy). upgrade node only updates that node’s local static pod manifests and kubelet config.

):Why one at a time? With stacked etcd, each control plane node runs its own etcd member, and etcd needs a quorum (majority) to work. Upgrading nodes one by one keeps a majority alive.

Tie this to your 2-master setup (Q17 in my list):

With 2 etcd members, quorum is 2, so you can tolerate zero failures.
When one master is being upgraded, its etcd restarts, you briefly lose quorum, and the API can become unavailable for writes.
A 2-node control plane is therefore no more fault-tolerant than 1 node for etcd. The recommendation is 3 control plane nodes, which tolerate 1 failure.

):The API stays reachable through the load balancer. During the upgrade, apiservers may be one minor version apart, which skew policy allows.”

):Extra points:

If etcd is external, kubeadm doesn’t upgrade it, so you upgrade it separately.
The load balancer in front of the apiservers keeps clients working while each node restarts.



